#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120690} :activity/id #uuid "711b406b-35cb-5fdc-b1b0-d63789565aba" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048823 :topic/id #uuid "c321a6fc-a622-5c4d-89a5-d91936b9920c" :topic/math-academy-id 1856} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121041 :tutorial/content "Remember that the null space $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ of a matrix $A$ consists of the solutions to the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}.$ To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the matrix ![](images/70/702794c0129df672cc7c4cc65260c9bfa7e33804ea9f71bba048cf62142a0a18.svg) Computing the reduced row echelon form of $A,$ we get\n\n![](images/16/16d52c068c616e2eca149a8e81929a98c85726361d7ea2291ab314fe7567e4ce.svg)\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system\n\n![](images/bb/bb8743bd8eb072476af0e9e31c0215916beeeb82fc371348de1e6013b38810f8.svg)\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ are basic variables (corresponding to the pivot columns) and $\\color{red}x_3$ is a free variable (corresponding to the non-pivot column).\n\nTherefore, any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n![](images/6e/6e124e2c030f6cad72932d09211935b438f1189322ded40683a1f18618e35f3e.svg)\n\nSo, the set ![](images/24/2449d6abf866e2baa123e81c1d610940930c75c6b45ecf818d36c62191ef605f.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ generates the null space of $A.$\n\n**Note:** It might be the case that $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ for a particular matrix $A$ equals the so-called zero subspace $\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}$ containing only the zero-vector. The basis of $\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}$ is said to be the *empty set*, which is a set that does not contain any vectors. The empty set is denoted by $\\emptyset$ or $\\{\\,\\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "892648a7-3b42-5d92-9592-1a8859cc6b0c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3207 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6cd915cb-1be5-57c4-9174-958e51421e23" :step/math-academy-id 17508 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119816 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119627 :question/id #uuid "1c4e02bb-5ec0-572e-92ba-46d16c6f03fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-4819"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cba14b5-62f7-5fbe-beb1-9b1511305cc6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433767}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Null Space of a Matrix Given a Row Equivalent Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "f601bb80-6906-5220-b7c7-22b3ad93f773" :step/math-academy-id 17509 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120694}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119628 :question/id #uuid "1cbcbca0-c8b5-55c9-af93-9b9c0ba59ffb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4820"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e66c269-233b-57f8-9dae-46eb78c228bc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433764}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for a Given Null Space"} :step/id #uuid "3a4d6c4c-5b51-5273-8870-97758393eeff" :step/math-academy-id 17510 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120693}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119818 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119629 :question/id #uuid "a9d2ed82-2ba5-53a6-973d-c859ea5c295f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4821"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c70963f-589d-532b-9286-559f68693df7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433965}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for the Null Space of a Matrix Given in Reduced Row Echelon Form"} :step/id #uuid "f391ffe0-615c-5d48-86e2-243cac8338e5" :step/math-academy-id 17511 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120691}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119630 :question/id #uuid "ef3be538-d974-5371-bd5b-a7e2ef3df827" :question/math-academy-id "e-4822"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd5a675-c692-505e-900b-d7500fb7c653" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418282}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for the Null Space of a Matrix That Is Not in Reduced Row Echelon Form"} :step/id #uuid "65f64dc2-9935-58c4-a4d2-93ee03872a6f" :step/math-academy-id 17512}] :activity/title "Finding a Basis of the Null Space of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120050}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433966} :activity/id #uuid "4586137b-2f21-5dd9-9f92-f73895c531d1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048823 :topic/id #uuid "c321a6fc-a622-5c4d-89a5-d91936b9920c" :topic/math-academy-id 1856} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3634eb4a-2da6-506c-862a-4df1259f8573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433949} {:answer/id #uuid "72404e91-0b79-5fbe-bf3b-a415c48e7a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433952} {:answer/id #uuid "89907820-6a08-564d-b8ae-b65ce2c3fedd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433953} {:answer/id #uuid "92e4bcf7-630e-5d3c-a886-783b15806935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433954} {:answer/id #uuid "a713ed5b-5694-581a-b057-41bc0b0505a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{\\,\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433953} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e52557d-0afb-5f71-8fd0-5b680e7b62a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "64b2528a-be45-5f89-bf42-907c121c9d08" :question/math-academy-id "q-74999" :question/problem "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nThe given matrix is already in row echelon form. So, the equation $Ax=0$ corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd) and ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable corresponding to the non-pivot column. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}\\in R.$ This implies that\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate the $\\text{Null}(A).$"} :step/id #uuid "34826f51-bff5-576f-8e59-d94eb0f524b1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433968}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433967 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bfcd48e-1e57-5c21-853e-08ad7ac4a5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433950} {:answer/id #uuid "664c9ed3-862f-5378-872f-f842ae26bbab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433951} {:answer/id #uuid "95fafc91-7cf2-5717-8af6-6f0822454343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433955} {:answer/id #uuid "d6697aa2-fa9e-539f-96c0-14b882489af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{\\,\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433958} {:answer/id #uuid "df170ecc-0149-5c63-b255-b0c96ff08d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433960}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433950} :answer-field/id #uuid "1876c4ef-5464-51fa-81fc-f9807976cbf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4bcb849a-6acb-581b-82ad-febe08cc464f" :question/math-academy-id "q-111399" :question/problem "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation $Ax=0$ corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{4}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=8{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th) and ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable corresponding to the non-pivot column. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}\\in R.$ This implies that\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate the $\\text{Null}(A).$"} :step/id #uuid "54aec285-4fb2-5f72-9458-615e815400c0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433968 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345022 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "038d8f66-0fdf-5eca-b0da-b68e8724c6db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{\\,\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433947} {:answer/id #uuid "11fe67bf-961a-57c3-a494-79893e59e352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433948} {:answer/id #uuid "9846224a-bf7a-5c09-b38f-8a30ce03496d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433956} {:answer/id #uuid "d816478a-9661-5854-a6c9-b2d73c0f3b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433959} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc395a0-e334-5fc2-b91b-ac21c50346f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433961} :answer-field/id #uuid "f161998d-b322-5952-9ff6-f21c3c0f3666" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "edf8482c-d2e1-5075-b974-a0f2a06f53e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-75026" :question/problem "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}+2{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{2}=-5{x}_{3}+{x}_{4},$ where ${x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are free variables. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4} \\\\ -5{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$"} :step/id #uuid "8b0318dc-2ed5-55c8-99e2-ef561cb961f3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433967}}] :activity/title "Finding a Basis of the Null Space of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433946}]}
